# DTF tier manifest: audit-admin-denial-full-path
# (artifact-keeper#5603, fixed by artifact-keeper#5831)
# Profile-set: filesystem. The refusal and its audit row happen in
# `admin_middleware`, ahead of any handler or storage I/O, so the storage
# dimension is irrelevant.
#
# THE BUG
# `admin_middleware` sits on the `/api/v1/admin` nest. When an authenticated
# non-admin reached an admin-only route it answered 403 and wrote a
# PERMISSION_DENIED audit row, but it took the path from `request.uri()`,
# which inside a nest is only what is left after the nest prefix. A refused
# `GET /api/v1/admin/settings` was recorded with `details.path` = `/settings`,
# so an auditor reading the row could not tell which surface was probed (and a
# `/settings` under another nest would look identical). The fix reads the
# `OriginalUri` extension and records `/api/v1/admin/settings`.
#
# Oracle (discriminating, RELEASE_GATE=1):
#   (CONTROL) a non-admin's GET /api/v1/admin/settings and
#             GET /api/v1/admin/audit answer 403; the admin's GET of the same
#             routes answers 200; each refusal writes exactly one
#             PERMISSION_DENIED row for that user (read back through
#             GET /api/v1/admin/audit?action=PERMISSION_DENIED&resource_id=
#             <user id>) with details.method GET and
#             details.reason admin_privileges_required. True on both images.
#   (FIX #5603) the rows record details.path /api/v1/admin/settings and
#             /api/v1/admin/audit. RED on main 2bdf0923: /settings and /audit.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: an admin login, a user create, a user login and a
# burst of audit polls; the base default (ON) could turn a poll into a 429.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
